// REJECTED — the stdlib-named-method sliver (roadmap 75(b)): a method call on
// a receiver whose provenance no constructor pins must not lower as that
// builtin. `open_pool` is an extern whose return carries no type, so
// `pool.remove("k")` used to lower as the Map `remove` builtin and dispatch
// against whatever the host actually returned — wrong at runtime on every
// tier, and invisible to every tier's compiler (the same hole `m.putt(k)`
// used to be, on the other side of the table). Only a receiver the checker
// can *prove* is a Str/List/Int/Int32/Bytes/Map value may call a stdlib
// method; an unpinned receiver is refused with the HOST-METHOD diagnostic,
// so the fence closes to exactly "host-object results are on the audit
// surface" (G8, docs/contract-errata.md "Typing gaps").
//
// expected error:
//   stdlib method `remove` on a value of unknown type

extern pure fn open_pool(name: Str) = @py { return object() }

fn f() {
  let pool = open_pool("pg://")
  pool.remove("k")
}
